  data:  # 5200
    kind: invariant  # 5200
    title: "Schema fields populated with one constant value, with zero entropy, or with name collisions across scopes"  # 5200
    status: violated  # 5200
    priority: 2  # 5200
    parent:  # 5200
    tags:  # 5200
    - dogfood_corpus_20260528  # 5200
    - dogfood_integrated  # 5200
    - dogfood_severity_moderate  # 5200
    isbefore: []  # 5200
    duplicate_of: []  # 5200
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 5200
    pr_ref:  # 5200
    description: "Several declared schema fields populate with effectively one value across every populated record — a constant. On edge-level metadata, 9 fields have zero entropy when populated. On node-level metadata, another 8 fields have zero entropy. Separately, 5 metadata field names appear on both `edge.meta` and `node.meta` with disjoint value distributions — the same name carries different vocabularies depending on which record type it appears on, and the dual-scope semantics are undocumented.\n\n### Specific symptoms confirmed on hypergumbo self-analysis\n\n**(1) 9 `edge.meta` fields are constant-value-only (zero entropy when populated).** Each carries exactly one value across all populated rows: `channel_kind='queue'` (103 occurrences), `detection_pattern='table_name'` (17), `table_name='users'` (17), `disambiguation_fallback=True` (11), `dest_access_mode='read'` (113), `protocol='ws'` (12), `bridge_kind='ffi'` (10), `client_framework='native_websocket'` (12), `url_path='/ws'` (12). Each field exists in the schema as if categorizing or routing, but stores a constant. Root cause: schema includes optional decoration fields populated by linkers (websocket-linker, message-queue-linker, database-query-linker, dependency-linker); each linker writes a constant value because it only emits one mechanism. Either the field should be derived from `origin` / `edge.type`, or other-value cases need linker support.\n\n**(2) 8 node-level fields have zero entropy.** `supply_chain.is_example_file=False` (33830/33830), `supply_chain.is_generated_file=False` (33830/33830), `meta.external_boundary=True` (1624 populated), `meta.package_ecosystem='npm'` (2), `meta.dependency_scope='dev'` (2), `meta.parent_base_classes=['IService']` (1), `meta.name='htrac-frontend'` (1), `meta.config_format='tsconfig'` (1). Combined with the 9 edge.meta constants above, 17 total zero-entropy fields across the schema. The `is_example_file` / `is_generated_file` fields probably reflect dead-code paths in supply_chain heuristics that never fire on this codebase; single-row meta fields (`name`, `config_format`, `parent_base_classes`) are likely tied to specific framework/manifest emitters that emit one record only.\n\n**(3) 5 meta field names collide between `edge.meta` and `node.meta` with disjoint value distributions.** The names: `query_type`, `queue_type`, `topic`, `topic_type`, `url_path`. Edge.meta has 22 distinct keys; node.meta has 42 distinct keys; these 5 appear in both. Concrete example: `url_path` on edges is `{/ws: 12}`; on nodes is `{/api/users: 10, /api/users/1: 1, /api: 1}` — entirely disjoint value sets. `queue_type` on edges peaks at `kafka` (85); on nodes peaks at `kafka` (55) but with `redis` (9) appearing at node-scope only. The same field name carries the same semantic concept but different mechanical scopes: edge.meta describes the edge relationship; node.meta describes the node. The schema treats edge.meta and node.meta as namespace-separate so the collision doesn't cause runtime issues, but it introduces conceptual confusion — two independent emitters (linker-side edge.meta vs analyzer-side node.meta) coined the same name for related-but-distinct semantics, and the schema does not document the dual-scope distinction.\n\n### Invariant\n\nA schema field that consistently carries one value either should be promoted to a constant in the schema (not a per-record field) or should be expanded by additional linker/emitter coverage to carry the variation it implies. Duplicate field names across record types should either share a vocabulary (and be documented as such in the schema) or be renamed to distinguish their scopes (e.g., `edge_url_path` vs `node_url_path`). Schema discoverability requires that declared fields predict the per-record variation a consumer will observe.\n\n### Scope\n\n17 zero-entropy fields total (9 edge.meta + 8 node.meta). 5 cross-scope field-name collisions. Affects both edge.meta and node.meta surfaces across the whole behavior map.\n\n### Exposure\n\n`behavior_map.edges[].meta.*` and `behavior_map.nodes[].meta.*` per-record metadata fields; schema documentation; consumers parsing meta-field vocabularies.\n\n### Consequence\n\nConsumers see schema bloat that conveys no per-record information: 17 fields that look like categorization but always carry the same value. Cross-scope field-name collisions silently mis-interpret as the same vocabulary — a consumer that merges `edge.meta.url_path` and `node.meta.url_path` joins disjoint distributions. Field discoverability is poor because the schema's declared fields do not predict per-record variation.\n\n### Candidate fixes\n\n- For each zero-entropy field, decide: (a) promote to a constant elsewhere in the schema, (b) extend linker support to emit the other expected values, or (c) remove the field entirely. The decision is per-field; document the rationale in each linker.\n- For the 5 cross-scope colliding field names, either explicitly document the dual-scope semantics in the schema or rename one side (the edge-side or the node-side) to disambiguate.\n- Add a property test that asserts every declared optional field with >50 populated rows has entropy > 0 (catches future regressions that introduce new constant-value fields).\n- Add a schema-level annotation distinguishing \"scoped only to edges,\" \"scoped only to nodes,\" or \"shared vocabulary across both\" for any meta field name used in both record types.\n\n### Subsumed (do not chase)\n\nThis item subsumes INV-raguv, INV-lokok, INV-vufov — which are deleted or will be deleted shortly and should only be referenced in an emergency."  # 5200
    fields:  # 5200
      dogfood_anon_id: "ITEM-c09542"  # 5200
      statement: "A schema field that consistently carries one value either should be promoted to a constant in the schema (not a per-record field) or should be expanded to carry the variation it implies. Duplicate field names across record types should either share a vocabulary or be renamed to distinguish their scopes."  # 5200
      root_cause: "Several declared schema fields populate with effectively one value across every populated record — a constant. One scope has a substantial set of metadata fields where every populated record carries the same value, suggesting either vestigial scaffolding from a partially-implemented feature or fields that should be derived rather than stored. A second scope (node-level rather than edge-level) has a similar set of zero-entropy fields. A separate but related issue: a handful of metadata field names appear on both the edge-level and node-level metadata records, with disjoint value distributions on each. The same field name carries different vocabularies depending on which record type it appears on; the dual-scope semantics are undocumented."  # 5200
